1
00:00:07,959 --> 00:00:10,120
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:10,121 --> 00:00:13,560
Starting this time, we'll begin the detailed rendering.

3
00:00:13,561 --> 00:00:17,960
First, let's do the skin rendering of the face and hands in this picture.

4
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:26,970
For reference, I split the parts for the lecture, but it's right to work evenly across the whole thing.

5
00:00:28,510 --> 00:00:31,961
To render in detail, let me increase the picture size a bit.

6
00:00:33,585 --> 00:00:38,371
Around seven to eight thousand is appropriate, based on height.

7
00:00:44,970 --> 00:00:50,090
Let me zoom in and begin rendering.

8
00:00:50,091 --> 00:00:54,650
I drew up to last time and came back after sleeping, and looked at the picture again.

9
00:00:54,651 --> 00:00:57,891
The moment I saw it, I wanted to move the face a bit to the right.

10
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:00,570
So I moved it.

11
00:01:00,571 --> 00:01:04,010
When you look at it after some time has passed, what to do suddenly becomes apparent.

12
00:01:05,234 --> 00:01:08,661
If you send the picture to your smartphone and look at it, it also looks different.

13
00:01:09,869 --> 00:01:14,110
That's one of the methods I often use to see the picture from a different perspective.

14
00:01:36,959 --> 00:01:37,960
Let me render with lines.

15
00:01:37,961 --> 00:01:41,000
The nose seems a bit too high, so I'll lower it.

16
00:01:41,001 --> 00:01:43,800
In cases like this, the clone stamp tool is convenient.

17
00:01:45,425 --> 00:01:47,610
The shortcut is S.

18
00:01:47,611 --> 00:01:54,611
After selecting the clone tool, hold Alt and click the nose. The part you clicked now becomes a stamp source.

19
00:01:56,539 --> 00:01:59,750
Release Alt, go down a little, and paint.

20
00:01:59,979 --> 00:02:01,870
This way you can move it easily.

21
00:02:07,715 --> 00:02:11,710
I keep rendering.

22
00:02:11,711 --> 00:02:17,511
When rendering a woman's face, I tend to use a soft brush rather than a hard one.

23
00:02:18,524 --> 00:02:21,521
It's good to render a female face surface softly.

24
00:02:58,204 --> 00:03:02,401
After carving the stone, the face got a bit small, so I made it bigger.

25
00:03:08,534 --> 00:03:11,680
Let me begin rendering the eyes, nose, and mouth.

26
00:03:11,681 --> 00:03:16,800
Since the big masses are established, just drawing in the lines makes it look decent.

27
00:03:22,390 --> 00:03:24,920
I'll paint the pupils later.

28
00:03:24,921 --> 00:03:26,120
Like applying makeup as a point.

29
00:03:44,094 --> 00:03:46,410
There are so many layers, my hands are really busy.

30
00:04:19,670 --> 00:04:21,850
I establish the flow of the hair.

31
00:04:21,851 --> 00:04:22,970
With lines, of course.

32
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:49,600
I'll render the ears too.

33
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:54,720
Since it's a full-body drawing, in cases like the left where the whole body is visible, things like ears aren't very visible.

34
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:12,750
Just paint it enough to know there are ears.

35
00:05:12,751 --> 00:05:14,990
Let me render the highlights on the hair.

36
00:05:16,565 --> 00:05:23,911
You can render with a brush like this, but rubbing it with the mixer brush tool like this is easier and more efficient.

37
00:05:38,590 --> 00:05:42,000
Let me zoom in and render a bit more.

38
00:05:42,001 --> 00:05:43,760
I'm drawing with a very thin brush.

39
00:05:46,225 --> 00:05:51,610
Even if you draw on the face like a doodle like this, if you look at the left side, you can't even tell.

40
00:05:51,611 --> 00:05:54,931
Think of it as lines that plan the coloring.

41
00:05:56,950 --> 00:06:01,160
Referencing these lines, add more paint.

42
00:06:01,161 --> 00:06:04,881
If you keep repeating this process, at some point it'll be finished.

43
00:06:46,065 --> 00:06:49,891
The face is too front-facing, so I wanted to turn it just a little.

44
00:06:51,505 --> 00:06:55,330
This time, I use the clone stamp tool I used earlier.

45
00:06:56,485 --> 00:07:01,200
Click while holding Alt, then move it a bit and paint.

46
00:07:01,201 --> 00:07:03,600
It moved subtly.

47
00:07:03,601 --> 00:07:06,640
There's another method I often use too.

48
00:07:06,641 --> 00:07:08,800
I duplicate the face with the lasso tool.

49
00:07:12,410 --> 00:07:15,900
This selected part gets duplicated.

50
00:07:15,901 --> 00:07:22,620
But the problem with this is the boundary is too sharp, so when I move it, the boundary isn't natural.

51
00:07:23,105 --> 00:07:25,780
Let me tell you how to solve this.

52
00:07:26,725 --> 00:07:31,440
Go back to lasso selection, right-click, and click Feather.

53
00:07:35,770 --> 00:07:37,500
It says feather radius.

54
00:07:37,501 --> 00:07:40,981
The higher this number, the softer the boundary becomes when selected.

55
00:07:42,295 --> 00:07:45,840
Let me try it.

56
00:07:45,841 --> 00:07:47,120
It changed too softly.

57
00:08:00,980 --> 00:08:02,030
Let me compare while decreasing the number.

58
00:08:02,031 --> 00:08:07,790
It differs by picture size, but for a local area like this, it'd be good to use around five to ten.

59
00:08:07,791 --> 00:08:09,310
You can see the boundary got softer, right?

60
00:08:11,125 --> 00:08:14,710
After duplicating like this, use the arrow keys to move it.

61
00:08:26,780 --> 00:08:30,190
Move the eyebrow the same way.

62
00:08:37,050 --> 00:08:41,100
Shall we compare?

63
00:08:41,179 --> 00:08:44,460
The face turned a bit, just as I wanted.

64
00:08:44,461 --> 00:08:45,461
Great.

65
00:09:16,430 --> 00:09:19,390
I keep rendering.

66
00:09:19,391 --> 00:09:20,590
A bit S.F.T.

67
00:09:20,591 --> 00:09:22,430
In that style, I drew this on the jaw, like this.

68
00:09:23,770 --> 00:09:29,861
Rather than holding onto this face rendering constantly, it's good to render it in the middle, as you draw the picture.

69
00:09:49,635 --> 00:09:52,750
I'll also render the hands.

70
00:09:52,751 --> 00:09:55,470
Zooming in, the sketch isn't tidied up.

71
00:09:56,005 --> 00:09:57,070
Let me tidy it up.

72
00:10:01,020 --> 00:10:05,150
For the hand, it's good to draw while referencing my own hand each time.

73
00:10:05,151 --> 00:10:13,390
At this time too, rather than taking a photo and drawing it exactly, it's better for learning to look briefly, draw, look briefly, draw.

74
00:10:13,391 --> 00:10:17,390
And while referencing my own hand, I need to beautify it.

75
00:10:17,470 --> 00:10:25,310
I often reference my own hand, so there were times when even drawing a woman, it came out like a man's hand.

76
00:10:25,311 --> 00:10:26,311
You have to be careful.

77
00:11:26,815 --> 00:11:30,970
The hand, like the face, has an expression.

78
00:11:30,971 --> 00:11:35,050
Before drawing the pose, I told you to act out the pose with your own body.

79
00:11:35,849 --> 00:11:37,930
The hand is the same too.

80
00:11:37,931 --> 00:11:42,570
The feeling in the hand's expression is just as important as the feeling from the facial expression.

81
00:11:47,555 --> 00:11:51,390
Do you know the character Scarlet Witch from the Marvel Cinematic Universe?

82
00:11:52,670 --> 00:11:56,281
The hand gestures when she uses magic are detailed and flashy.

83
00:11:57,240 --> 00:12:01,770
I hear she received training from a choreographer separately for the character's expression.

84
00:12:01,771 --> 00:12:03,530
Because they valued the hand's expression.

85
00:12:07,830 --> 00:12:11,761
I too draw while valuing the hand's expression.

86
00:13:35,290 --> 00:13:39,020
I'm redrawing because I don't like the hand.

87
00:13:39,100 --> 00:13:45,900
When modifying a picture like this, I also maintain the process of sketching, base color, and coloring consistently.

88
00:13:49,775 --> 00:13:54,840
Why do I put the ring finger and pinky together like this?

89
00:13:54,841 --> 00:13:59,400
Should I say I'm applying the concentrate and release approach to the hand too?

90
00:13:59,401 --> 00:14:05,560
Or, if all five fingers are spread apart, it might look too wrinkled.

91
00:14:05,561 --> 00:14:11,201
I get the feeling an alien visiting Earth would think human hands look very wrinkled.

92
00:14:12,945 --> 00:14:16,691
I draw them with a long, extended feel.

93
00:15:00,270 --> 00:15:02,860
Long nails help with the finger length.

94
00:15:28,220 --> 00:15:29,220
If you do that.

95
00:15:57,985 --> 00:15:59,820
I keep rendering.

96
00:16:04,090 --> 00:16:07,411
Once the hand has progressed a bit, let's look at the face again.

97
00:16:08,600 --> 00:16:13,210
Looking after time has passed like this, you might see points to fix.

98
00:16:13,240 --> 00:16:14,810
I'll continue with more.

99
00:16:16,465 --> 00:16:19,370
I don't draw with the thought of finishing.

100
00:16:19,371 --> 00:16:23,130
From my experience, I keep touching the face until right before the picture is complete.

101
00:16:24,540 --> 00:16:28,990
Even after completion, if I see a strange part, I take it out again and fix it.

102
00:16:34,110 --> 00:16:37,641
Without much explanation, I'll keep rendering the face and hands.

103
00:21:43,855 --> 00:21:47,770
I'll also render the armpit area.

104
00:21:47,771 --> 00:21:51,971
Think of it as rendering the two muscles, the biceps and the chest muscle.

105
00:21:53,979 --> 00:21:56,300
If you know the muscle forms, it's not hard.

106
00:22:32,059 --> 00:22:33,060
Hmm.

107
00:22:54,689 --> 00:22:58,141
Let me render the hair a bit more too.

108
00:23:00,319 --> 00:23:04,460
For the flowing hair expression, paint so there's value contrast.

109
00:23:06,254 --> 00:23:11,160
If you've grasped the head's shape and characteristics, you can draw this easily too.

110
00:23:12,444 --> 00:23:17,400
As I mentioned earlier, expressing the broad grain with the mixer brush tool makes it easier.

111
00:23:27,739 --> 00:23:32,220
Drawing these fine hairs too can make it look even more realistic.

112
00:24:24,524 --> 00:24:28,780
The face seemed a bit red, so I slightly adjusted the hue.

113
00:24:30,870 --> 00:24:33,960
I'll open the hue and saturation window and make adjustments.

114
00:24:33,960 --> 00:24:35,720
The shortcut is Control and U.

115
00:24:37,399 --> 00:24:40,611
I moved the hue part a bit to the right.

116
00:24:41,529 --> 00:24:44,170
Compared, there's a subtle difference.

117
00:26:05,659 --> 00:26:10,620
This brush I'm using now is one I said I used a lot in the past.

118
00:26:10,621 --> 00:26:12,780
It's a brush that leaves grain, like drawing with charcoal.

119
00:26:13,794 --> 00:26:18,460
I'm using it like cleaning the surface with a water-moistened brush when sculpting.

120
00:26:20,694 --> 00:26:35,440
It's also good for getting rid of harsh leftover sketch marks.

121
00:26:35,441 --> 00:26:38,480
This is how I did the skin rendering.

122
00:26:38,481 --> 00:26:42,720
Honestly, if you keep digging, you can render endlessly.

123
00:26:42,721 --> 00:26:48,560
Concept art is really a battle against time, so you can't hold onto it indefinitely, but even so, like this.

124
00:26:48,689 --> 00:26:51,641
This way, a rendering that ends within an hour is no burden.

125
00:26:52,600 --> 00:26:58,090
In the case of an illustration, you'd hold onto it for hours or even days.

126
00:26:58,091 --> 00:27:01,731
You'd draw considering every line and every touch.

127
00:27:02,749 --> 00:27:08,870
It's good to grasp the purpose of the picture and decide how far to render, then do it.

128
00:27:31,579 --> 00:27:35,381
Next time, we'll get into the detailed rendering of the clothing.
